          "derivation": "*ǵʰu-tó- is the regular zero-grade verbal adjective (the -to- participle) of *ǵʰewH-, hence literally 'the (one) invoked, the called-upon' — Pokorny writes it 'as *ghu-to-m angerufenes Wesen' and Watkins 'Suffixed zero-grade form *ghu-to-, the invoked, god'. The Indo-Aryan witness for the same participle is Vedic hu-tá- 'invoked', an epithet of Indra (Klein cites it under god), so the formation is not an ad hoc reconstruction. Nourai's gloss 'God, one who is called upon for help' is Watkins's gloss expanded. The only reservation worth recording is that Wiktionary notes the laryngeal in *ǵʰewH- ought to give a long vowel (*ǵʰuH-tó- > *gūda-), which is one of the reasons the Germanic word is still argued about (see node #5); the reconstruction itself, as the -to- adjective of this root, is standard.",
